================================================================================
  1.  WHAT THIS CRATE IS
================================================================================

A single Rust crate that provides, for server-side use:

  - A from-scratch implementation of ML-KEM-768 and ML-KEM-1024
    (FIPS 203), including Keccak, field arithmetic, NTT, CBD
    sampling, byte encoding, the IND-CPA PKE, the IND-CCA KEM,
    and the FIPS 203 section 7.2 / 7.3 key-validation routines.

  - A hybrid X25519 + ML-KEM KEM in the X-Wing style. The
    construction is documented in full and pinned by a
    known-answer test. It is NOT byte-compatible with X-Wing.

  - A secret-derived "room" protocol: a group key derived from
    passphrases or other out-of-band sources via Argon2id, with
    a forward-secure symmetric ratchet, optional ML-DSA signatures
    on messages, and a rekey mechanism that upgrades a room to
    post-compromise security by wrapping fresh randomness to
    members' KEM public keys.

  - Chunked large-file encryption using the STREAM construction,
    with format v1 (ML-KEM-768 only) and v2 (any suite: ML-KEM-768,
    ML-KEM-1024, or either X25519 hybrid).

  - ML-DSA-65 and ML-DSA-87 signatures via the RustCrypto
    ml-dsa crate (not rewritten here).

  - Passphrase-based encryption of arbitrary private key material
    using Argon2id + ChaCha20-Poly1305, with a versioned blob
    format that authenticates its own KDF parameters.

  - Key wrapping: encapsulate to a recipient's public key, derive
    a domain-separated AEAD key, encrypt a payload, return a
    self-describing binary blob or a JSON structure.

The crate is #![forbid(unsafe_code)]. Every key material type
implements Drop and zeroizes on drop. Sensitive intermediates are
zeroized after use in the functions that produce them.


================================================================================
  2.  WHAT CHANGED IN 0.7.0
================================================================================

This release doubles the scope of the crate relative to 0.3.0. The
changes are additive - no public API that existed in 0.3.0 was
removed or changed in a breaking way - but they are substantial
enough that a reader who last saw 0.3.0 needs a map.

NEW IN 0.7.0

  - ML-KEM-1024 support. The KEM is now generic over the parameter
    set. params::KemLevel enumerates both levels with their FIPS
    203 sizes; kem::*_level functions take a level argument;
    MlKemKeyPair::new_with_level and from_seed_with_level produce
    either level.

  - FIPS 203 section 7.2 / 7.3 key validation.
    kem::check_ek_level rejects encapsulation keys whose
    coefficients are not all below q.
    kem::check_dk_level additionally verifies the stored hash
    H(ek) against the embedded ek. Both are exposed through
    validate_encapsulation_key and are called by encapsulate_to,
    FileDecryptor::new, and wrap_room_key_for_user.

  - X25519 + ML-KEM hybrid KEM (hybrid module). Domain-separated
    seed expansion, transcript-bound combiner, low-order X25519
    point rejection on the sender side. Pinned by a Python-model
    KAT.

  - Secret-derived rooms (room module). Argon2id-stretched
    sources -> root -> room id, auth token, epoch 0 key.
    Per-sender hash chains with a bounded skip window. Optional
    ML-DSA signatures on messages and on rekey bundles. Rekey
    injects fresh randomness wrapped to members' KEM public keys,
    so a leaked passphrase does not compromise future epochs.

  - File format v2. Records the suite id in the header so that
    ML-KEM-1024 and hybrid files are distinguishable from the
    original ML-KEM-768-only format. Files encrypted to
    ML-KEM-768 keys are still written as v1, so older readers
    keep working.

  - Passphrase blob v2. The Argon2id parameters are stored in
    the header and authenticated as AAD, so an attacker cannot
    weaken them by tampering. Legacy v1 blobs (with default
    parameters and no header) are still accepted on decrypt.

  - Room key wrapping v2. The wrapped-key AEAD key is now derived
    by hashing the KEM ciphertext, the recipient public key, and
    the shared secret with a domain separator. v1 blobs (raw
    shared secret as AEAD key) can still be opened.

  - Zeroization throughout. All key material types, all
    intermediates that contain key material, and all decrypted
    buffers are zeroized on drop or after use.

  - KAT files and independent models. See section 9.

BEHAVIOUR CHANGES THAT MAY AFFECT CALLERS

  - wrap_room_key_for_user now emits {"v":2,...} and the ciphertext
    is bound to the recipient's public key. Old v1 JSON can be
    decrypted by passing version = 1 to unwrap_room_key.

  - MlKemKeyPair::import_keys now rejects an ek that does not match
    the dk_seed, rather than accepting any well-formed ek.

  - FileDecryptor::new rejects a decapsulation key whose embedded
    hash does not match. This is a correctness check, not a
    performance check.

  - The CipherboxError type is unchanged in shape. New error
    strings appear for the new checks.


================================================================================
  3.  WHAT IS IN THIS CRATE
================================================================================

WRITTEN FROM SCRATCH IN THIS CRATE

  keccak.rs
      Keccak-f[1600] permutation. SHA3-256, SHA3-512, SHAKE-128,
      SHAKE-256. Tested against NIST-published vectors and
      against Python's hashlib.

  field.rs
      Field arithmetic modulo q = 3329. Montgomery
      multiplication with R = 2^16. QINV is derived at compile
      time by brute-force search for the value satisfying
      (candidate * q) & 0xFFFF == 1, so no transcription error
      is possible.

  ntt.rs
      The 7-layer NTT and inverse NTT. Zetas table is computed at
      compile time from zeta = 17 and the bit-reversal permutation,
      so the constants are provably correct.

  poly.rs
      CBD sampling for eta = 2.
      Compression for d = 1, 4, 10, 11, 12.
      Byte encoding for the same widths.
      Division by q is implemented as multiply-and-shift (the
      KyberSlash mitigation); the exactness of the constant is
      exhaustively tested for every d in [1, 12] and every x
      in [0, q).

  pke.rs
      IND-CPA PKE (K-PKE.KeyGen / Encrypt / Decrypt).
      Generic over the parameter set.

  kem.rs
      IND-CCA KEM (ML-KEM.KeyGen / Encaps / Decaps).
      Fujisaki-Okamoto transform with implicit rejection.
      FIPS 203 section 7.2 and 7.3 key checks.
      Generic over the parameter set; fixed-size ML-KEM-768
      wrappers preserved for compatibility.

  params.rs
      Parameter-set and suite descriptors, with sizes that match
      FIPS 203 Table 2 and are checked by test.

  hybrid.rs
      X25519 + ML-KEM hybrid KEM. See section 6.

  file.rs
      Chunked file encryption (STREAM). See section 8.

  room.rs
      Secret-derived rooms with a forward-secure ratchet.
      See section 7.

  lib.rs
      Public API surface: MlKemKeyPair, MlDsaKeyPair,
      MlDsa87KeyPair, HybridKeyPair, Decapsulator trait,
      passphrase encryption, key wrapping, room re-exports.

PROVIDED BY DEPENDENCIES, NOT REWRITTEN

  - ML-DSA-65 and ML-DSA-87 signatures (via ml-dsa)
  - ChaCha20-Poly1305 AEAD (via chacha20poly1305)
  - Argon2id password hashing (via argon2)
  - X25519 (via x25519-dalek)
  - base64 encoding (via base64)
  - OS entropy (via getrandom)
  - zeroization (via zeroize)


================================================================================
  4.  WHAT IS NOT IN THIS CRATE
================================================================================

Read this before section 5. It is ordered by how much each item
should affect your decision to use the crate.

TRUST BARRIERS - these should stop you from using the crate in
production today

  Not audited.
      There has been no independent third-party security audit.
      No external review of the code, the constructions, or the
      file formats. Do not deploy this crate to protect anything
      that matters until an audit has been performed. If you
      need an audited ML-KEM today, use the RustCrypto ml-kem
      crate, which has been security-assessed.

  Not ACVP-validated for ML-KEM.
      The KEM is validated against an OpenSSL-derived KAT and
      against OpenSSL-generated vectors that cover the same
      algorithm. The NIST ACVP test vectors are not currently in
      tests/vectors/, so this crate does not claim FIPS 203
      conformance. If you need to make an ACVP conformance
      claim today, use a library that passes the ACVP suite.

      This is the single largest gap relative to the earlier
      versions of this crate, and it is a gap this project can
      close: restoring tests/vectors/ and tests/acvp.rs brings
      the crate back to ACVP-validated status. Until then, treat
      the claim as "independently validated, not ACVP-certified."

THREAT MODEL BOUNDARIES - these describe what the crate does not
protect against

  No constant-time analysis on the compiled binary.
      The field arithmetic is written to be constant-time with
      respect to input values, and the KyberSlash division has
      been removed from the source. But no timing measurement
      has been performed on the compiled binary, no formal
      verification has been performed on any function, and no
      masking, shuffling, or blinding has been applied to any
      arithmetic. A local attacker who can measure fine-grained
      timing on the machine running this crate is outside the
      model this crate claims to defend.

  WebAssembly and JIT are not analysed at all.
      If you compile this crate to wasm32, or run it under a JIT
      that can reorder instructions, the timing behaviour is not
      characterised. Do not use this crate in environments where
      an attacker can observe timing and you cannot control the
      compiler.

  No protection against a compromised passphrase.
      The room protocol and the passphrase encryption both
      depend on a passphrase or an out-of-band source. If that
      secret leaks, an attacker who recorded traffic can decrypt
      epoch 0 of a room and any blob encrypted with that
      passphrase. Room::rekey upgrades a room to post-compromise
      security for later epochs, but only if the rekey happens
      before the attacker uses the leaked secret.

SCOPE - these are absent by design, not by omission

  No network protocol.
      This crate provides primitives and file formats. It does
      not implement transport, framing, authentication of peers,
      discovery, or session establishment. Those belong in the
      application that uses this crate.

  No ML-DSA implementation.
      ML-DSA-65 and ML-DSA-87 signatures use the RustCrypto
      ml-dsa crate. They are not rewritten here. If your threat
      model requires signatures to be from-scratch as well, this
      crate does not meet it. The ML-KEM and Keccak are from
      scratch; the signatures are not.

  No X25519 implementation.
      The hybrid KEM uses x25519-dalek. If your threat model
      requires the hybrid to be entirely from scratch, this
      crate does not meet it. The ML-KEM half is from scratch;
      the X25519 half is not.

  No certificate handling, no key management, no rotation
  policy.
      This crate produces keys and ciphertexts. It does not
      manage their lifecycle. Storing, rotating, revoking, and
      distributing keys and passphrases is the application's
      responsibility.

  No interoperability with X-Wing or HPKE.
      The hybrid construction is documented and pinned by KAT,
      but it is not byte-compatible with X-Wing, and there is no
      HPKE integration. If you need to interoperate with an
      existing deployment that uses either, this crate is not
      the right building block.

WHAT THIS CRATE IS DESIGNED FOR, GIVEN THE ABOVE

  The crate is designed for three use cases:

    Reference and teaching.
        A small, readable ML-KEM with a fully specified file
        format and room protocol, validated against independent
        implementations. Useful when the goal is to understand
        or to audit the construction, not to deploy it.

    A building block you intend to audit or fork.
        If you are going to read the code, run the KATs, and
        make a decision about it yourself, this crate gives you
        something to read.

    A format you want to reimplement elsewhere.
        The file and room formats are fully specified in
        sections 7 and 8. If you need the format but not the
        Rust code, the specification is here.

  If none of those describes your use case, the RustCrypto
  ml-kem crate is the right choice for ML-KEM, and the ml-dsa
  crate is the right choice for signatures. See section 11.


