================================================================================
  6.  HYBRID KEM CONSTRUCTION
================================================================================

The hybrid KEM combines X25519 and ML-KEM so that the shared secret
is secure if either holds.

Seed expansion (one 32-byte seed -> both halves):

  (d || z || x25519_sk) = SHAKE-256(
      "cipherbox-hybrid-v1" || level_id || seed32, 96)

Public key:

  ek_mlkem || x25519_pk
      1216 bytes for ML-KEM-768 hybrid
      1600 bytes for ML-KEM-1024 hybrid

Ciphertext:

  ct_mlkem || x25519_ephemeral_pk
      1120 bytes for ML-KEM-768 hybrid
      1600 bytes for ML-KEM-1024 hybrid

Shared secret:

  ss = SHA3-256(
      "cipherbox-hybrid-v1" || level_id
      || ss_mlkem || ss_x25519
      || ct_mlkem || ct_x25519
      || ek_mlkem || x25519_pk)

The combiner hashes the whole transcript - both shared secrets,
both ciphertexts, and the entire public key - so the result is
bound to this exact key and this exact exchange. The seed
expansion is domain-separated by level, so the same seed
produces unrelated keys at the two levels.

Sender-side: an X25519 public key that produces an all-zero
shared secret (a low-order point) is rejected.

Receiver-side: the X25519 result is included in the hash
unconditionally, so a malformed ciphertext simply produces an
unrelated secret rather than an error.

This is the X-Wing construction style. It is NOT byte-compatible
with X-Wing - the domain strings and the seed expansion differ.
Do not mix this crate's hybrid public keys with X-Wing
implementations.


================================================================================
  7.  ROOM PROTOCOL
================================================================================

A room is identified and keyed by a secret that members obtain out
of band (a passphrase, the hash of a photo, a QR code, or several
of these combined). No server-side member list, invitation, or
key distribution is required.

Derivation:

  k_i     = Argon2id(source_i, salt = H(context, params))
            per source
  root    = H("root", context, params, n, sort(k_1 .. k_n))
  room_id = H("room-id", root)
  auth    = H("auth", root)
  epoch_0 = H("epoch0", root)

The order of sources does not matter. context (a server name, a
room label) separates unrelated rooms that share sources. auth is
a bearer token; the server should store only auth_verifier(auth),
not auth itself.

Messages:

  Every sender has a 16-byte id and a per-epoch hash chain.

  chain_0    = H("chain0", epoch_key, sender_id, epoch)
  msg_key_n  = H("msg-key", chain_n)
  nonce_n    = H("msg-nonce", chain_n)[..12]
  chain_n+1  = H("chain-next", chain_n)

Each message key is used once and the chain key is overwritten
after use. A device compromised later cannot decrypt messages it
already processed, provided the root secret itself was not
retained or leaked.

Ratchet state advances only after a message authenticates, so
garbage cannot be used to burn message keys. Skipped messages are
tracked in a bounded window (MAX_SKIP); messages beyond the window
are refused rather than buffered.

Forward secrecy properties:

  Against state compromise: yes.

  Against leak of the root secret (a stolen passphrase): no. That
  is inherent to secret-derived rooms. Use Room::rekey to inject
  fresh randomness wrapped to members' KEM public keys; later
  epochs then stay secret even if the root leaks. This is
  post-compromise security and is the reason the rekey function
  exists.

Sender authenticity:

  Optional. seal_signed attaches an ML-DSA signature to the
  envelope. open with a verifier rejects unsigned messages and
  messages whose signature does not verify, before any ratchet
  state is touched. Without signatures, any holder of the root
  can impersonate any sender id.

Envelope format (version 1):

  "CBRM" | 0x01 | flags u8 | room_id 32 | epoch u32 LE
  | sender_id 16 | index u32 LE
  | ct_len u32 LE | ChaCha20-Poly1305 ciphertext
  | [flags & 1: sig_len u16 LE | signature]

The signature, when present, covers:

  "cipherbox-room-v1/msg-sig" || header || ct_len || ct

Rekey bundle format (version 1):

  "CBRK" | 0x01 | room_id 32 | epoch u32 LE | n u32 LE
  | (recipient_fingerprint 32 | len u32 LE | wrapped seed)*
  | sig_len u16 LE | signature

The signature covers:

  "cipherbox-room-v1/rekey-sig" || room_id || epoch || n || entries

rekey requires a signer, and apply_rekey requires a verifier. A
bundle that is not signed by a key the caller trusts is rejected.
This is what stops someone who merely knows the room secret from
hijacking the room.

State export is a self-describing binary blob:

  "CBRS" | 0x01 | ...

Encrypt it before storing - it contains the ratchet state, which
is secret.


================================================================================
  8.  FILE FORMAT
================================================================================

Chunked encryption of arbitrarily large files using the STREAM
construction.

Header, version 1 (ML-KEM-768 only):

  "CBOX" | 0x01 | chunk_size u32 LE | kem_ct (1088)

Header, version 2 (any suite):

  "CBOX" | 0x02 | suite_id u8 | chunk_size u32 LE | kem_ct

Suite ids:

  3     ML-KEM-768
  5     ML-KEM-1024
  0x13  X25519 + ML-KEM-768
  0x15  X25519 + ML-KEM-1024

For hybrid suites, kem_ct is ct_mlkem || ct_x25519.

Key derivation:

  aead_key = SHA3-256(domain || header || shared_secret)

  domain        = "cipherbox-file-v1" for v1
                  "cipherbox-file-v2" for v2
  header        = every byte from magic through the end of kem_ct
  shared_secret = 32 bytes from the KEM

Because the header is part of the KDF input, modifying any header
byte makes every chunk fail authentication.

Chunk framing:

  ChaCha20-Poly1305 with nonce

  counter (u64 LE) || 00 00 00 || last_flag

  last_flag = 0x01 for the final chunk, 0x00 otherwise
  AAD = "CBOX-v1-chunk"

The last chunk is always present and may be empty. Stripping
trailing chunks leaves a final chunk authenticated with
last_flag = 0, which fails when decrypted as the last chunk.

Streaming:

  Both FileEncryptor and FileDecryptor accept arbitrary-sized
  slices. Every possible split point of the plaintext or
  ciphertext across update calls produces the same output. This
  is tested exhaustively.

Truncation, reordering, tampering:

  Truncation   Detected at finish by the last_flag mismatch.
  Reordering   Detected because the nonce contains the chunk
               counter, so a chunk decrypted with the wrong
               counter fails its AEAD tag.
  Header tampering
               Detected because the header is bound into the
               AEAD key derivation.
  Body tampering
               Detected per chunk by the AEAD tag.

Partial plaintext is never written to the output on failure.


================================================================================
  9.  TESTING AND VALIDATION
================================================================================

Test files:

  tests/keccak_kat.rs    3 tests
      SHA3-256/512, SHAKE-128/256. NIST-published vectors and
      hashlib-generated vectors for multi-block inputs.

  tests/kat.rs           3 tests
      ML-KEM-768 and ML-KEM-1024 KATs from OpenSSL.
      File-format KATs from an independent Python model.

  tests/hybrid.rs        17 tests
      Hybrid KATs, keygen/encaps/decaps, low-order rejection,
      file encryption to hybrid keys.

  tests/room.rs          19 tests
      Room derivation, ratchet, signatures, rekey, state
      persistence. KAT from an independent Python model.

  tests/integration.rs   23 tests
      End-to-end across KEM, file, wrap, passphrase, and ML-DSA
      at both levels.

  Inline unit tests      15 tests
      Field arithmetic, NTT, poly encoding, compress/decompress.

Total: 80 tests, all passing.

Independent models:

  Keccak vectors from NIST and Python hashlib.
  ML-KEM vectors from OpenSSL (via python-cryptography).
  Hybrid and file vectors from an independent Python model that
  uses OpenSSL-validated ML-KEM and RFC 7748 X25519.
  Room vectors from an independent Python model that uses hashlib
  SHA3, python-cryptography Argon2id (checked against RFC 9106),
  and python-cryptography ChaCha20-Poly1305.

What is not covered:

  NIST ACVP vectors for ML-KEM. Not currently in tests/vectors/.

Reproducing:

  cargo test --all-targets
  cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings


================================================================================
 10.  SECURITY MODEL AND HONEST LIMITATIONS
================================================================================

WHAT IS VERIFIED

  - Round-trip correctness of every cryptographic primitive in
    every suite.

  - Constant-time behaviour of field arithmetic with respect to
    input values (no data-dependent branches, no division, no
    table lookups) in the source code. Not verified on the
    compiled binary.

  - Implicit rejection in the Fujisaki-Okamoto transform -
    invalid ciphertext produces a deterministic pseudorandom
    value, not an error.

  - Key validation per FIPS 203 section 7.2 and 7.3 before any
    encapsulate or decapsulate call touches an untrusted key.

  - Chunked file format detects truncation, reordering, header
    tampering, and body tampering.

  - Room ratchet cannot be advanced by forged envelopes;
    signatures are checked before any state change.

  - Zeroization of all key material types on drop, and of
    intermediates after use.

WHAT IS NOT VERIFIED

  - Independent third-party audit. There has been none.

  - Formal verification. No mechanized proof of correctness.

  - Side-channel resistance beyond what the source code does.
    No timing measurements on the compiled binary. No masking,
    shuffling, or blinding.

  - WebAssembly and JIT environments. Not analysed at all.

WHEN TO USE THIS CRATE

  - You want to understand ML-KEM, the hybrid construction, or a
    symmetric ratchet by reading a small, self-contained
    implementation.

  - You want a transparent, KAT-validated building block that you
    can audit or fork.

  - You want a fully specified chunked file format that you can
    implement in another language if you need to.

  - You are learning or teaching.

WHEN NOT TO USE THIS CRATE

  - You need a certified, audited, production-grade KEM. Use a
    library that has been certified or audited.

  - You need ACVP conformance today. This crate does not
    currently claim it.

  - You need features this crate does not provide (ML-DSA from
    scratch, network protocol, certificate handling, etc.).


================================================================================
 11.  COMPARISON TO RUSTCRYPTO AND OTHER PROJECTS
================================================================================

RustCrypto ml-kem
-----------------

  The RustCrypto ml-kem crate is what most Rust applications
  should use for ML-KEM. It is security-assessed, maintained,
  and used in production. This crate exists for a different
  purpose: transparency at the algorithm level, and a fully
  specified file and room layer on top of it. If you are
  building a product, use ml-kem. If you are building a
  reference implementation or an auditable system, use this.

Polynom
-------

  Polynom is a closed-source collaboration platform that uses
  Kyber-1024 and Dilithium5, plus a proprietary entropy-based
  key exchange layer. It is a complete product (chat, rooms,
  file sharing, mobile apps). This crate is a toolkit: a KEM,
  a hybrid, a file format, and a room protocol. Different users,
  different trust model.

  Where this crate differs from Polynom:
    - Uses ML-KEM-768 (Level III) as the default, with
      ML-KEM-1024 available.
    - Uses ChaCha20-Poly1305 instead of AES-256-GCM.
    - Publishes KAT vectors generated from independent
      implementations.
    - Publishes the file and room formats.

  Where Polynom differs from this crate:
    - Is a complete product with a UI and network protocols.
    - Uses a proprietary entropy layer that is not a NIST
      standard.

liboqs
------

  liboqs is the reference C library for post-quantum
  cryptography. It implements many more algorithms than this
  crate, and is the library used by OpenSSL's PQC support. If
  you need breadth, use liboqs. This crate is narrower:
  from-scratch ML-KEM with a fully specified hybrid, file
  format, and room protocol on top.


================================================================================
 12.  BUILDING
================================================================================

Requirements:

  Rust 1.85 or later (edition 2024).
  No C compiler, no system libraries.

Build:

  cargo build --release

Run tests:

  cargo test --all-targets

Run clippy:

  cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings

Full CI:

  cargo fmt --all -- --check
  cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings
  cargo test --all-targets


================================================================================
 13.  DEPENDENCIES
================================================================================

  getrandom 0.2
      OS entropy.

  ml-dsa =0.1.2
      ML-DSA-65 and ML-DSA-87 signatures. Pinned for
      reproducibility; contains the fix for CVE-2026-24850.

  chacha20poly1305 0.10
      AEAD for file chunks, room messages, key wrapping, and
      passphrase blobs.

  argon2 0.5
      Argon2id for passphrase and room source stretching.

  base64 0.22
      Encoding for CLI and JSON formats.

  zeroize 1
      Zeroization on drop.

  x25519-dalek 2
      X25519 for the hybrid KEM.

None of these is a KEM. The ML-KEM implementation and the hybrid
combiner are entirely in this crate.


